# =====================================================================
# TecGate — environment template
# =====================================================================
#
#     curl -o .env https://raw.githubusercontent.com/amslertec/tecgate/main/.env.example
#
# Then fill in the three values below.
#
# `docker compose` reads a file called `.env` from the directory it runs
# in. This template is deliberately NOT called that, so that nobody ends
# up running the template as their configuration. Rename it (the `-o` in
# the command above does that) or copy it:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# All three appear in `docker-compose.yml` as `${...:?...}`. If one is
# missing, `docker compose up` stops with the sentence behind it instead
# of quietly carrying on with an empty string. An empty ACME_EMAIL would
# otherwise only surface when Let's Encrypt refuses the first
# certificate — weeks later, somewhere that has nothing to do with this
# file.
#
# The values below are PLACEHOLDERS from ranges reserved for
# documentation (RFC 1918, RFC 2606). They work nowhere, and that is
# intentional: a template that happens to run never gets filled in.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The mail address of the Let's Encrypt account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Expiry warnings go there. Let's Encrypt does not check the address for
# deliverability — a made-up address is accepted, and the warning then
# never arrives.
ACME_EMAIL=admin@example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# This machine's address on your own network
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Becomes LOKALE_DOCKER_ADRESSE: the address at which TRAEFIK reaches
# this machine's containers.
#
# **Not `localhost` and not `127.0.0.1`.** Traefik runs in a container;
# from where it stands, `localhost` is the container and not the
# machine. A proxy host pointing at a local container would go nowhere
# — and only at request time, not at startup.
LAN_ADDRESS=192.168.1.10

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Additional names for the interface's self-signed certificate
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Becomes ZERT_NAMEN: the container variable TecGate itself reads. The
# key here and the variable there differ on purpose — this file is what
# an operator types, the variable is what the program was built around.
#
# TecGate's own TLS listener (:8091) issues its certificate itself. The
# container only ever sees its own network — the name or address under
# which the interface is actually called can only be told to it.
# Separate several with commas; usually the same value as LAN_ADDRESS.
#
# If the name the interface is really called under is missing, the
# browser warns on every visit — and one gets used to a warning one
# stops reading.
CERT_NAMES=192.168.1.10

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: which image version to run
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `docker-compose.yml` uses ${TECGATE_VERSION:-latest}. Leave this line
# commented out and you follow `latest`. Pin it to a released version if
# you would rather decide yourself when the software changes underneath
# you — `docker compose pull` then does nothing until you edit this line.
#
# TECGATE_VERSION=latest

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required: the numeric id of the host's docker group
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Since version 0.1.0 the container runs as an unprivileged user (uid
# 1000) instead of root. That closes an obvious hole — code running
# inside the container is no longer root inside it — but it costs one
# piece of setup, because two things root got for free now need saying
# out loud.
#
# This is the first: the `dockerwacht` service reads /var/run/docker.sock
# to list your containers. The socket is owned by root and by a group
# called "docker", and that group's number differs from host to host.
# Find it with:
#
#     getent group docker | cut -d: -f3
#
# Get it wrong and nothing crashes — the container page simply stays
# empty, and the log says "permission denied".
DOCKER_GID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required once: hand the data directory to that user
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This is the second, and it is not a variable but a command you run once
# before the first start:
#
#     sudo chown -R 1000:1000 ./data
#
# The container writes its database, its Traefik configuration and
# acme.json there. As root it owned those files; as uid 1000 it cannot
# touch them until you say so. Upgrading from a version that ran as root?
# Then run it too — your existing files are owned by root.
